RULE-000 — Coverage Classification
Rule ID: RULE-000
Rule Name: Coverage Classification
Rule Type: Pre-Analysis / Coverage Classification
Execution Priority: FIRST — MANDATORY
Applies To: All CoverageIQ D&O policy and quote analyses


⸻


1. Purpose
Before CoverageIQ performs any coverage optimization analysis, it must first determine which coverage modules are actually being provided by the uploaded policy or quote.
CoverageIQ is designed primarily for D&O and Management Liability documents. These documents may contain multiple coverage modules within the same policy or quote.
Rule 000 must independently evaluate whether the document provides:
Private Company D&O
Employment Practices Liability (EPLI)
Fiduciary Liability
Crime
The output of Rule 000 determines which downstream optimization skills are permitted to execute.
CoverageIQ must classify purchased/provided coverage—not merely identify coverage language contained within a policy form.


⸻


2. Mandatory Full-Document Review
CoverageIQ must review the entire available document before finalizing Rule 000.
Review all available components, including:
Declarations
Quote/declaration pages
Coverage schedules
Premium schedules
Limits
Retentions/deductibles
Insuring agreements
Coverage parts
Endorsements
Forms schedules
Definitions
Exclusions
Sublimits
Notices
Any indication that coverage is selected, purchased, included, declined, or not applicable
CoverageIQ must not finalize coverage classification based only on the declarations page or first few pages.
It may develop preliminary classifications while processing the document, but classifications become final only after the entire available document has been reviewed and relevant evidence reconciled.


⸻


3. Coverage Modules
CoverageIQ must independently classify each of the following:
D&O
Search semantically for concepts including:
Directors & Officers Liability
Directors and Officers Liability
D&O
D&O Liability
Private Company D&O
Private Company Management Liability
Management Liability — D&O
Executive Liability
Executive Protection
Directors, Officers and Company Liability
Organization Liability
Company Liability
Coverage-specific D&O limits, retentions, premiums, coverage selections, and endorsements should be evaluated as evidence.
Important: The mere presence of Side A, Side B, or Side C language does not establish that D&O coverage was purchased.


⸻


EPLI
Search semantically for concepts including:
Employment Practices Liability
Employment Practices Liability Insurance
EPL
EPLI
Employment Liability
Employment Practices Coverage
Wrongful Employment Practices
Employment Practices Wrongful Acts
Also evaluate EPLI-specific:
Limits
Retentions
Premiums
Insuring agreements
Endorsements
Third-Party EPL provisions
Employment-related exclusions
Coverage schedules


⸻


Fiduciary Liability
Search semantically for concepts including:
Fiduciary Liability
Fiduciary Liability Insurance
Fiduciary Coverage
ERISA Fiduciary Liability
Employee Benefit Plan Fiduciary Liability
Benefit Plan Fiduciary Liability
Fiduciary Wrongful Act
Also evaluate coverage-specific limits, premiums, retentions, insuring agreements, endorsements, and coverage parts.


⸻


Crime
Search semantically for concepts including:
Crime
Commercial Crime
Crime Coverage
Crime Insurance
Fidelity
Employee Theft
Employee Dishonesty
Computer Fraud
Funds Transfer Fraud
Forgery or Alteration
Crime may be structured differently from D&O, EPLI, and Fiduciary.
CoverageIQ should recognize that Crime may consist of multiple separately scheduled insuring agreements, limits, or sublimits.


⸻


4. Evidence Collection
For each of the four coverage modules, CoverageIQ must collect affirmative, negative, and supporting evidence before assigning a final classification.
The agent should not simply count keyword occurrences.
It must evaluate what the language means in context.


⸻


5. Evidence Hierarchy
Tier 1 — Affirmative / Conclusive Evidence
Strong evidence that a coverage is actually provided includes:
Coverage + Limit
Examples:
Directors & Officers Liability — $2,000,000
Employment Practices Liability — $1,000,000
Fiduciary Liability — $1,000,000
Employee Theft — $500,000
A clearly identified coverage-specific limit is strong affirmative evidence.


⸻


Coverage Selection
Examples:
Included
Purchased
Covered
Selected
Coverage Provided
Applies
Yes
when clearly associated with the applicable coverage module.


⸻


Coverage + Premium
Examples:
D&O Premium — $7,500
EPLI Premium — $4,500
Fiduciary Premium — $2,000
A premium explicitly allocated to a specific coverage module is strong affirmative evidence that the coverage is provided.
However, premium structure must be evaluated according to the Combined Premium rules below.


⸻


Coverage-Specific Declarations or Schedule
A declarations page or coverage schedule affirmatively showing a coverage module as included is strong evidence.


⸻


Affirmative Coverage Endorsement
An endorsement explicitly adding or activating a coverage module is strong evidence.


⸻


6. Tier 2 — Strong Supporting Evidence
Supporting evidence includes:
Coverage-specific retention
Coverage-specific deductible
Coverage-specific insuring agreement
Coverage-specific endorsement
Coverage-specific sublimit
Coverage-specific exclusions
Coverage-specific definitions
Coverage-specific claims provisions
Examples:
EPLI Retention — $50,000
D&O Retention — $25,000
Fiduciary Retention — $10,000
These strongly support the existence of the corresponding coverage but should be reconciled with Tier 1 evidence whenever available.


⸻


7. Tier 3 — Incidental Evidence
Examples include:
Side A/B/C wording appearing in a generic modular form
Generic references to Directors or Officers
Generic references to Employees
ERISA terminology appearing in exclusions or definitions
Crime terminology appearing incidentally
Defined terms for coverage modules not actually purchased
Generic insuring agreements included within an unselected modular form
Tier 3 evidence must never independently establish that coverage is PRESENT.


⸻


8. Combined / Package Premium Handling
This is a mandatory classification guardrail.
Management Liability policies and quotes may provide multiple coverage modules under a single combined premium.
For example:
Management Liability Package Premium — $18,500
could potentially apply to:
D&O
EPLI
Fiduciary
or another combination of coverages.
Therefore:
The number of premiums displayed must never be used to determine the number of coverage modules provided.
CoverageIQ must not reason:
“Only one premium exists, therefore only one coverage exists.”
Likewise, CoverageIQ must not reason:
“A Management Liability premium exists, therefore every Management Liability coverage is included.”
A combined premium confirms only what the document affirmatively associates with that premium.
When a package premium exists, CoverageIQ must continue searching for coverage-specific evidence such as:
Limits
Retentions
Coverage selections
Coverage schedules
Insuring agreements
Endorsements
Sublimits
Declarations
Core Principle
Premium structure and coverage structure must be evaluated independently.


⸻


9. Retention Handling
Different coverage modules may have different retentions within the same policy.
Example:
D&O — $25,000 Retention
EPLI — $50,000 Retention
Fiduciary — $10,000 Retention
These are strong indicators that multiple coverage modules may be provided.
However, CoverageIQ should not assume coverage solely because a retention appears somewhere in a generic form.
The retention must be reasonably associated with the applicable coverage module.


⸻


10. Negative Evidence
CoverageIQ must actively search for evidence indicating that coverage is not provided.
Examples:
Not Purchased
Not Covered
Not Included
Not Selected
Declined
N/A
No Coverage
$0 Limit
Coverage Part Not Applicable
Explicit negative evidence generally overrides incidental policy-form language.
Example:
EPLI — NOT PURCHASED
combined with a later EPLI insuring agreement appearing in the generic policy form does not establish EPLI coverage.
The correct classification is generally:
EPLI → NOT_PRESENT
unless contradictory affirmative evidence requires MANUAL_REVIEW.


⸻


11. Evidence Reconciliation
CoverageIQ must evaluate the document as a whole.
Do not classify coverage immediately upon encountering the first relevant term.
Instead:
Identify Evidence
↓
Record Evidence
↓
Continue Full-Document Review
↓
Identify Conflicting Evidence
↓
Reconcile Evidence
↓
Finalize Classification
Later endorsements may modify, add, remove, or clarify coverage identified earlier in the document.


⸻


12. Conflict Handling
If CoverageIQ finds conflicting evidence, it must not choose whichever evidence appears more persuasive without sufficient support.
Example:
Declarations:
EPLI — Not Purchased
Later endorsement:
Employment Practices Liability Coverage Added
CoverageIQ should investigate whether the endorsement affirmatively modifies the declarations.
If the relationship cannot reliably be determined:
🟡 MANUAL_REVIEW
Provide both pieces of conflicting evidence.


⸻


13. Classification Status Logic
Every coverage module must receive one of three classifications.
🟢
PRESENT
Sufficient affirmative evidence establishes that the coverage is provided.
CoverageIQ must cite the strongest evidence supporting the classification.


⸻


⚪
NOT_PRESENT
CoverageIQ has sufficient evidence that the coverage is not provided.
Examples include:
Explicitly marked Not Purchased
Explicitly marked Not Covered
Explicitly declined
Complete coverage schedule clearly showing the module is not selected
The absence of a keyword alone is not sufficient to establish NOT_PRESENT.


⸻


🟡
MANUAL_REVIEW
CoverageIQ cannot reliably determine whether coverage is provided.
Examples:
Conflicting evidence
Ambiguous coverage schedule
Coverage language exists but purchase cannot be established
Limits cannot reliably be associated with coverage
Missing declarations
Missing endorsements
Poor document quality
Incomplete quote
Conflicting endorsements
Unclear package premium allocation
CoverageIQ must explain why manual review is required.


⸻


14. Important Classification Guardrails
CoverageIQ must never assume:
Side A/B/C language → D&O is purchased
Employment definitions → EPLI is purchased
ERISA terminology → Fiduciary is purchased
Employee Theft language → Crime is purchased
Management Liability policy → all four modules are purchased
One premium → one coverage
Package premium → all available modules are purchased
No evidence found → coverage definitely absent
Classification must be based on the actual structure of the transaction reflected by the document.


⸻


15. Downstream Rule Gating
Rule 000 controls which optimization modules execute.
If D&O =
PRESENT
Execute:
DO-001
DO-002
DO-003
DO-004
…and future D&O rules.
If EPLI =
PRESENT
Execute:
EP-001 onward.
If Fiduciary =
PRESENT
Execute:
FI-001 onward.
If Crime =
PRESENT
Execute:
CR-001 onward.
If Coverage =
NOT_PRESENT
Do not execute that coverage’s optimization rules.
If Coverage =
MANUAL_REVIEW
Do not silently treat the coverage as either present or absent.
Flag the classification for broker review and suppress coverage-specific optimization conclusions that depend on a definitive classification unless administrator configuration expressly provides otherwise.


⸻


16. Required Output — Coverage Snapshot
Rule 000 should produce a Coverage Snapshot before any optimization recommendations are displayed.
Example:
Coverage Snapshot
🟢 Private Company D&O
Status: PRESENT
Limit: $2,000,000
Retention: $25,000
🟢 EPLI
Status: PRESENT
Limit: $1,000,000
Retention: $50,000
⚪ Fiduciary Liability
Status: NOT_PRESENT
🟢 Crime
Status: PRESENT
Employee Theft: $500,000
Computer Fraud: $250,000


⸻


17. Evidence Requirements
Every classification must provide supporting evidence.
For each coverage module return:
Coverage:
Status: PRESENT / NOT_PRESENT / MANUAL_REVIEW
Limit(s):
Retention(s):
Premium: Coverage-Specific / Combined / Not Identified
Primary Evidence:
Supporting Evidence:
Negative Evidence:
Page(s):
Section / Endorsement:
Relevant Language:
Confidence: HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW
If a combined premium is identified, CoverageIQ should report it as Combined rather than incorrectly allocating it among individual modules.


⸻


18. Confidence Requirements
HIGH
Strong affirmative or negative evidence clearly establishes classification.
Example:
Employment Practices Liability
Limit: $1,000,000
Retention: $50,000
Coverage: Included
MEDIUM
Multiple supporting indicators strongly suggest classification, but direct evidence is less explicit.
LOW
Evidence is incomplete, ambiguous, or conflicting.
Low-confidence classification should generally result in:
MANUAL_REVIEW
rather than a definitive conclusion.


⸻


19. Validation Checklist
Before Rule 000 completes, CoverageIQ must verify:
☑ Entire available document reviewed
☑ D&O evaluated
☑ EPLI evaluated
☑ Fiduciary evaluated
☑ Crime evaluated
☑ Limits reviewed
☑ Retentions reviewed
☑ Premium structure reviewed
☑ Combined premiums handled appropriately
☑ Coverage schedules reviewed
☑ Applicable endorsements reviewed
☑ Negative coverage indicators reviewed
☑ Conflicting evidence reconciled
☑ Evidence captured for each classification
☑ Confidence assigned
☑ Applicable downstream modules selected
Only after completing this process may CoverageIQ proceed to optimization analysis.


⸻


RULE-000 Core Instruction
If I had to give the agent one overriding instruction, it would be:
Review the entire document and determine what coverage was actually purchased or provided. Do not classify coverage merely because corresponding policy language exists. Evaluate limits, retentions, premiums, coverage selections, declarations, schedules, insuring agreements, endorsements, exclusions, and other evidence together. A single combined premium may apply to multiple coverage modules and must never be interpreted as evidence that only one coverage exists. When evidence is insufficient or conflicting, return MANUAL_REVIEW rather than guessing.
I think Rule 000 is now strong enough to serve as the gatekeeper for the entire CoverageIQ system. The next thing I’d eventually want to do is stress-test it against maybe 10–20 actual carrier quotes/policies—especially modular management liability products—because that’s where we’ll discover carrier-specific classification edge cases we haven’t anticipated yet.
